1. Start by inspecting the existing repository and all relevant project files.
2. If a repository already exists, preserve its useful code and history. If starting from an empty repository, create a complete project from scratch.
3. Never replace functioning implementation with placeholders or simulated functionality.
4. Never report a feature as complete merely because its UI or source code exists.
5. Never fabricate successful actions, tests, screenshots, API responses, or build results.
6. Implement features incrementally and build the application after each meaningful stage.
7. Fix compiler errors and regressions before proceeding to dependent features.
8. Keep credentials out of source code, Git history, logs, and publicly accessible configuration files.
9. Do not expose or reuse previously disclosed API credentials. Require the user to rotate any exposed key.
10. Maintain a clear distinction between implemented, compiled, unit-tested, emulator-tested, and real-device-tested functionality.
11. Use real application state and actual Android APIs. No fake contacts, fake conversations, fake app launches, or simulated success.
12. Never bypass Android security restrictions, app authentication, user consent, or protected system interfaces.
13. Work directly on the project and commit real changes to GitHub when repository write access is available.
14. If a dependency, API, permission, or platform limitation blocks a feature, explain the limitation and implement the best supported alternative.
15. Do not stop after writing a plan. Execute the implementation, build, inspect errors, and continue fixing them until the available environment's limitations are reached.

