24. DEFINITION OF DONE

The project is not complete merely because it compiles.

A feature may be called implemented only when its source code exists and its dependencies are connected correctly.

A feature may be called build-verified only when the corresponding build succeeds.

A feature may be called test-verified only when the relevant tests actually pass.

A feature may be called device-verified only when it has been exercised on an actual Android device and the result has been observed.

The overall project is ready for a real-device trial when:

- The application builds successfully.
- Conversation and device-action requests are handled differently.
- The agent can open a real application.
- It can inspect the current accessible screen.
- It can perform supported actions and inspect the result.
- It can complete a bounded multi-step task or explain exactly why it cannot.
- Screen vision is genuinely connected when enabled.
- Consequential actions require approval.
- Voice failure has a usable fallback.
- Errors and cancellation are handled.
- CI results and APK artifacts are available.

Do not claim that every Android application is fully automatable. Some apps hide content, restrict accessibility, require user authentication, or block automated interaction.

